    },
    {
      "id": "zmZ1qthXNtl-1PzM3Ru0e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3181.94641487032,
      "y": -247.21404359944427,
      "width": 1823.8106666666683,
      "height": 1506.2120425029516,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ap",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1740500866,
      "version": 1459,
      "versionNonce": 141611659,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790698928187,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. How many users ? How fast They are growing\n2. Is the App is read heavy or write heavy ?\n3. What data you dont wanna loss and what data you can afford to lose ?\n4. How much latency you can afford and in which section read or write ?\n5. What does it cost\n\nNow the right system design is less cost with answer of above 4 questions.\n\nAfter understanding the requirements, we can choose an appropriate architecture.\n\nTwo common approaches are:\n\nMonolithic architecture\nMicroservices architecture\n\nMonolithic Architecture\n\nIn a monolithic application, the different features of the application are developed and\ndeployed as one application/unit which means every service code like auth , Feed , like ,\ncomment is in one single server\n\nThe important point is that monolith does not necessarily mean one physical server or exactly\none database. A monolithic application can run on multiple servers for scalability.\n\nMicroservices Architecture\n\nIn a microservices architecture, the application is divided into smaller, independently deployable\nservices, where each service is responsible for a particular business capability.\n\nDistributed system → components of the system run across multiple machines and communicate\nover a network.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. How many users ? How fast They are growing\n2. Is the App is read heavy or write heavy ?\n3. What data you dont wanna loss and what data you can afford to lose ?\n4. How much latency you can afford and in which section read or write ?\n5. What does it cost\n\nNow the right system design is less cost with answer of above 4 questions.\n\nAfter understanding the requirements, we can choose an appropriate architecture.\n\nTwo common approaches are:\n\nMonolithic architecture\nMicroservices architecture\n\nMonolithic Architecture\n\nIn a monolithic application, the different features of the application are developed and deployed as one application/unit which means every service code like auth , Feed , like , comment is in one single server\n\nThe important point is that monolith does not necessarily mean one physical server or exactly one database. A monolithic application can run on multiple servers for scalability.\n\nMicroservices Architecture\n\nIn a microservices architecture, the application is divided into smaller, independently deployable services, where each service is responsible for a particular business capability.\n\nDistributed system → components of the system run across multiple machines and communicate over a network.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "o7qcpa2qAhTSBMnYH0p9W",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5346.94641487032,
      "y": -367.21404359944427,
      "width": 2753.810666666669,
      "height": 194.34994096812278,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 520713154,
      "version": 1623,
      "versionNonce": 1087697602,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790619936425,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Microservices are useful when different parts of an application need to be developed, deployed, or scaled independently. We should not choose\nmicroservices simply because the application has a certain number of users.\n\nFor example, 1 lakh users/day does not automatically mean you need microservices. A well-designed monolith can handle significant traffic.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Microservices are useful when different parts of an application need to be developed, deployed, or scaled independently. We should not choose microservices simply because the application has a certain number of users.\n\nFor example, 1 lakh users/day does not automatically mean you need microservices. A well-designed monolith can handle significant traffic.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pseZYfJ5EgjaZmWlYTh11",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8388.226765187246,
      "y": -281.60185772560953,
      "width": 2803.8106666666695,
      "height": 1651.9744982290435,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 933557918,
      "version": 2037,
      "versionNonce": 2014109579,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790698917462,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When an application starts receiving more traffic, we need to increase its capacity. There are two common approaches:\n\nVertical Scaling\nHorizontal Scaling\n\n1. Vertical Scaling :- Vertical scaling means increasing the resources/specifications of an existing server. We are making the same server more\npowerful.\n\nProblems with Vertical Scaling\n  1. Hardware Limit :- There is a physical/instance limit to how much we can upgrade a single machine.You cannot keep increasing CPU and RAM\nforever.\n  2. Single Point of Failure :- If your entire application is running on one server, and that server goes down, the application goes down.\n  3. Cost :- Higher-spec machines can become increasingly expensive. For example, moving from a smaller instance to a much larger instance can cost\nsignificantly more than using several smaller instances.\n\n2. Horizontal Scaling :- Horizontal scaling means adding more servers/instances instead of making one server more powerful. The incoming requests\nare distributed among multiple servers.\n\nAdvantage with horizontal Scaling\n  1. Better Fault Tolerance :- If one server fails. The other servers can continue handling requests.\n  2. Can Handle Large Traffic :- Horizontal scaling is particularly useful when the number of requests becomes very large.\n  3. No Single Machine Limit :- Instead of being limited by the maximum capacity of one machine, we can add more machines.\n\nSometimes one powerful server is simpler, faster, and cheaper than managing 10 smaller servers. Horizontal scaling becomes attractive when the\nworkload, availability requirements, or growth make distributing the workload worthwhile.\n\nInterview Question :- \nYour application is getting slower. Why don't you just buy a bigger server?\"\n\nA good way to approach the answer is: First, understand the workload and bottleneck. If the application is relatively small and traffic is low,\nvertical scaling can be a simple solution because we can increase the server's resources without significantly changing the architecture.\n\nAs traffic grows, we may need horizontal scaling so that requests can be distributed across multiple servers and the system can provide better\navailability and capacity.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When an application starts receiving more traffic, we need to increase its capacity. There are two common approaches:\n\nVertical Scaling\nHorizontal Scaling\n\n1. Vertical Scaling :- Vertical scaling means increasing the resources/specifications of an existing server. We are making the same server more powerful.\n\nProblems with Vertical Scaling\n  1. Hardware Limit :- There is a physical/instance limit to how much we can upgrade a single machine.You cannot keep increasing CPU and RAM forever.\n  2. Single Point of Failure :- If your entire application is running on one server, and that server goes down, the application goes down.\n  3. Cost :- Higher-spec machines can become increasingly expensive. For example, moving from a smaller instance to a much larger instance can cost significantly more than using several smaller instances.\n\n2. Horizontal Scaling :- Horizontal scaling means adding more servers/instances instead of making one server more powerful. The incoming requests are distributed among multiple servers.\n\nAdvantage with horizontal Scaling\n  1. Better Fault Tolerance :- If one server fails. The other servers can continue handling requests.\n  2. Can Handle Large Traffic :- Horizontal scaling is particularly useful when the number of requests becomes very large.\n  3. No Single Machine Limit :- Instead of being limited by the maximum capacity of one machine, we can add more machines.\n\nSometimes one powerful server is simpler, faster, and cheaper than managing 10 smaller servers. Horizontal scaling becomes attractive when the workload, availability requirements, or growth make distributing the workload worthwhile.\n\nInterview Question :- \nYour application is getting slower. Why don't you just buy a bigger server?\"\n\nA good way to approach the answer is: First, understand the workload and bottleneck. If the application is relatively small and traffic is low, vertical scaling can be a simple solution because we can increase the server's resources without significantly changing the architecture.\n\nAs traffic grows, we may need horizontal scaling so that requests can be distributed across multiple servers and the system can provide better availability and capacity.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "XexNtQAIRLn44E-FD1rl9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 8422.393431853912,
      "y": 1820.8981422743907,
      "width": 2803.8106666666695,
      "height": 1360.4495867768594,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aph",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1228675781,
      "version": 2265,
      "versionNonce": 1755078187,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790699931688,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Basically, when we send a request, we usually send it over HTTP, which is a stateless protocol (a set of rules for communication).\n\nStateless means that each request is treated independently. The server does not automatically remember previous requests.\n\nHTTP generally uses port 80 for unencrypted connections. The secure version is HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure)**, which uses\n**TLS** to encrypt the communication and generally uses port 443.\n\nNow let's say we want to open: https://google.com We can type google.com in the browser, but the network doesn't communicate using the domain\nname directly. It needs an IP address to know which machine to communicate with. So the browser uses DNS (Domain Name System) to resolve:\ngoogle.com → IP address. Once we get the IP address, the request can be sent toward that destination over the internet. But this is not the\nend.\n\nThe request also contains information such as:\n\n* Protocol → HTTPS\n* Destination → google.com\n* Port → 443\n* HTTP method → GET\n* Path → `/`\n* Headers\n* Other request data, if required\n\nSo : -\nDNS finds the destination server.\nThe IP address helps packets reach that destination.\nThe port identifies the service.\nThe HTTP method + path help the server determine the endpoint/resource.\nAnd finally, the server sends the HTTP response back to the client.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Basically, when we send a request, we usually send it over HTTP, which is a stateless protocol (a set of rules for communication).\n\nStateless means that each request is treated independently. The server does not automatically remember previous requests.\n\nHTTP generally uses port 80 for unencrypted connections. The secure version is HTTPS (Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure)**, which uses **TLS** to encrypt the communication and generally uses port 443.\n\nNow let's say we want to open: https://google.com We can type google.com in the browser, but the network doesn't communicate using the domain name directly. It needs an IP address to know which machine to communicate with. So the browser uses DNS (Domain Name System) to resolve: google.com → IP address. Once we get the IP address, the request can be sent toward that destination over the internet. But this is not the end.\n\nThe request also contains information such as:\n\n* Protocol → HTTPS\n* Destination → google.com\n* Port → 443\n* HTTP method → GET\n* Path → `/`\n* Headers\n* Other request data, if required\n\nSo : -\nDNS finds the destination server.\nThe IP address helps packets reach that destination.\nThe port identifies the service.\nThe HTTP method + path help the server determine the endpoint/resource.\nAnd finally, the server sends the HTTP response back to the client.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wQNgf4g27jbC5wjcmMLa9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6075.726765187245,
      "y": 2324.231475607724,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 485.874852420307,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apj",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1442547909,
      "version": 2506,
      "versionNonce": 569576069,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790702750656,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When we use horizontal scaling, we have multiple servers handling our application, so we need something\nto distribute incoming requests among those servers. This is where a Load Balancer comes in.\n\nA load balancer is software running on a server or network infrastructure whose job is to distribute\nincoming requests across available servers using different algorithms, such as Round Robin, Least\nConnections, and Weighted Distribution. \n\nHowever, if we have only one load balancer, it itself can become a single point of failure. Therefore, in\nlarge-scale systems, multiple load balancers or redundant load balancing infrastructure can be used so\nthat if one fails, another can continue handling the traffic.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When we use horizontal scaling, we have multiple servers handling our application, so we need something to distribute incoming requests among those servers. This is where a Load Balancer comes in.\n\nA load balancer is software running on a server or network infrastructure whose job is to distribute incoming requests across available servers using different algorithms, such as Round Robin, Least Connections, and Weighted Distribution. \n\nHowever, if we have only one load balancer, it itself can become a single point of failure. Therefore, in large-scale systems, multiple load balancers or redundant load balancing infrastructure can be used so that if one fails, another can continue handling the traffic.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pVrHw7oBqjq0w8p4RH1Ue",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3901.0600985205783,
      "y": 2270.2314756077235,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 583.0498229043683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apk",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 150469349,
      "version": 2654,
      "versionNonce": 1788441061,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790702750656,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A stateless system means that each request is independent and does not depend on information stored\non a particular server from a previous request. For example, if we have 10 servers, any request from a\nuser can go to any of those servers because the required information is available with the request itself\nor in a shared data store.\n\nOn the other hand, a stateful system means that the server needs information from previous requests\nto process the current request. For example, if a user's login state is stored only on Server 1, the load\nbalancer may need to keep sending that user's requests to Server 1. If Server 1 goes down or the\nrequest goes to another server, the user may face problems. To make the system stateless, we can\nstore authentication information in a JWT and send it with each request. Each server can then\nindependently verify the JWT using the required secret/key, so the request does not depend on which\nparticular server receives it.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A stateless system means that each request is independent and does not depend on information stored on a particular server from a previous request. For example, if we have 10 servers, any request from a user can go to any of those servers because the required information is available with the request itself or in a shared data store.\n\nOn the other hand, a stateful system means that the server needs information from previous requests to process the current request. For example, if a user's login state is stored only on Server 1, the load balancer may need to keep sending that user's requests to Server 1. If Server 1 goes down or the request goes to another server, the user may face problems. To make the system stateless, we can store authentication information in a JWT and send it with each request. Each server can then independently verify the JWT using the required secret/key, so the request does not depend on which particular server receives it.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "1SGEzoAvueb7mBtu2T6jj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1751.0600985205774,
      "y": 2172.231475607723,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 825.9872491145218,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2012488587,
      "version": 2793,
      "versionNonce": 682666292,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790758801266,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "SQL vs NoSQL is not about what they can do. It's about which one makes the required workload easier,\nsafer, and more efficient given the trade-offs.\n\nNoSQL databases can often do the same business operation as SQL, but they don't necessarily provide\nthe same guarantees, query model, transaction model, or efficiency for that operation.\n\nSQL databases have traditionally been designed around relational data and strong transactional\nguarantees such as ACID transactions.\n\nNoSQL databases can also provide transactions and consistency features — modern NoSQL is much more\ncapable than the simple \"NoSQL = no transactions\" idea.\n\nE-commerce has important transactional operations such as orders, inventory, and payments. These\noperations have strong consistency and relationship requirements, so I would likely choose a relational\ndatabase for the transactional core. However, I could still use NoSQL for workloads such as product\ncatalogs, activity data, recommendations, or other areas where its data model and scaling\ncharacteristics are beneficial.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "SQL vs NoSQL is not about what they can do. It's about which one makes the required workload easier, safer, and more efficient given the trade-offs.\n\nNoSQL databases can often do the same business operation as SQL, but they don't necessarily provide the same guarantees, query model, transaction model, or efficiency for that operation.\n\nSQL databases have traditionally been designed around relational data and strong transactional guarantees such as ACID transactions.\n\nNoSQL databases can also provide transactions and consistency features — modern NoSQL is much more capable than the simple \"NoSQL = no transactions\" idea.\n\nE-commerce has important transactional operations such as orders, inventory, and payments. These operations have strong consistency and relationship requirements, so I would likely choose a relational database for the transactional core. However, I could still use NoSQL for workloads such as product catalogs, activity data, recommendations, or other areas where its data model and scaling characteristics are beneficial.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "q9bH2otKP_PqmkZLLeYvt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1739.0600985205774,
      "y": 3310.231475607723,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 777.3997638724911,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkd",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1640276748,
      "version": 3129,
      "versionNonce": 1198370356,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790759994463,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The decision is more about data structure, relationships, consistency, query patterns, scale, and access\nrequirements.\n\nBefore choosing a database, we first understand the application's requirements and ask questions such\nas:\n\n1. ) What type of data are we storing? like user , posts , comments and likes etc\n2. ) What does the data structure look like? like How user schema look like\n3. ) Are there relationships between different types of data? like there is a relation between posts and\nuser\n4. ) What queries will we perform most often? which query we use more write or read \n5. ) How important are transactions and strong consistency? A transaction in SQL is a group of one or\nmore database operations that are treated as a single unit of work. Either all operations are\nsuccessfully completed, or none of them are applied. \n6. ) How much data and traffic do we expect?\n7. ) Does the schema change frequently?",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The decision is more about data structure, relationships, consistency, query patterns, scale, and access requirements.\n\nBefore choosing a database, we first understand the application's requirements and ask questions such as:\n\n1. ) What type of data are we storing? like user , posts , comments and likes etc\n2. ) What does the data structure look like? like How user schema look like\n3. ) Are there relationships between different types of data? like there is a relation between posts and user\n4. ) What queries will we perform most often? which query we use more write or read \n5. ) How important are transactions and strong consistency? A transaction in SQL is a group of one or more database operations that are treated as a single unit of work. Either all operations are successfully completed, or none of them are applied. \n6. ) How much data and traffic do we expect?\n7. ) Does the schema change frequently?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "KpO4OfQjP_6dm7_TnfX2r",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -398.9399014794226,
      "y": 2143.0648089410565,
      "width": 2013.8106666666695,
      "height": 923.1622195985832,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apkl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1602722188,
      "version": 2869,
      "versionNonce": 1834091060,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790759382475,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Indexing is a technique used in databases to make reading and searching data faster. We can create an\nindex on a column that we frequently use for searching, filtering, sorting, or joining.\n\nUnder the hood, databases commonly use a B-tree or B+ tree to maintain the index, which allows the\ndatabase to find the required data without scanning every row of the table.\n\nA primary key is automatically indexed in most SQL databases, while a foreign key is not necessarily\nindexed automatically, so we may need to create an index explicitly if we frequently query using that\ncolumn.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, if the posts table has a user_id foreign key and we\nfrequently fetch all posts belonging to a user, we can create an index on user_id to make that query\nfaster.\n\nHowever, indexes also have a cost: they require additional storage and can make INSERT, UPDATE, and\nDELETE operations slower because the database also needs to maintain the index whenever the\nunderlying data changes.\n\nTherefore, we should create indexes based on actual query patterns rather than indexing every column.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Indexing is a technique used in databases to make reading and searching data faster. We can create an index on a column that we frequently use for searching, filtering, sorting, or joining.\n\nUnder the hood, databases commonly use a B-tree or B+ tree to maintain the index, which allows the database to find the required data without scanning every row of the table.\n\nA primary key is automatically indexed in most SQL databases, while a foreign key is not necessarily indexed automatically, so we may need to create an index explicitly if we frequently query using that column.\n\nFor example, in an Instagram-like application, if the posts table has a user_id foreign key and we frequently fetch all posts belonging to a user, we can create an index on user_id to make that query faster.\n\nHowever, indexes also have a cost: they require additional storage and can make INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE operations slower because the database also needs to maintain the index whenever the underlying data changes.\n\nTherefore, we should create indexes based on actual query patterns rather than indexing every column.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OqE-6pR7rA4BiO683rHy1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5335.279748203653,
      "y": -115.54737693277752,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 485.874852420307,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apl",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1408883778,
      "version": 1799,
      "versionNonce": 765778334,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790619944089,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reason 1 — Independent Scaling\n\nSuppose we have an Instagram like application and in this type of app Reading posts happens much more frequently than Writing posts happens\nmuch less frequently.\n\nWith monolith :- If the read traffic increases significantly, we may need to add more instances of the whole application. The problem is that\nwe're also replicating the parts responsible for writing and other functionality, even though they might not need additional capacity.\n\nWith Microservices :- We can separate the application into services Now, if read traffic increases, we can scale only the Read Service. This is\ncalled independent scaling.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Reason 1 — Independent Scaling\n\nSuppose we have an Instagram like application and in this type of app Reading posts happens much more frequently than Writing posts happens much less frequently.\n\nWith monolith :- If the read traffic increases significantly, we may need to add more instances of the whole application. The problem is that we're also replicating the parts responsible for writing and other functionality, even though they might not need additional capacity.\n\nWith Microservices :- We can separate the application into services Now, if read traffic increases, we can scale only the Read Service. This is called independent scaling.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5we-iQDqP3KB-9VCAmXeu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5335.279748203653,
      "y": 401.1192897338892,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 388.69988193624556,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apt",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 980770562,
      "version": 1913,
      "versionNonce": 1628764574,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790620168028,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reason 2 — Large Teams\n\nMicroservices can also make sense when an organization has many teams working on different parts of the system. For example: Team A → User\nService , Team B → Post Service \nEach team can own and deploy its service independently. With a huge monolithic codebase, many teams working on the same codebase can lead\nto: More coordination , More merge conflicts , More dependencies between teams , More difficult deployments. \nMicroservices can help teams work more independently. However, team size alone doesn't automatically mean you need microservices. A modular\nmonolith can also solve many of these problems.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Reason 2 — Large Teams\n\nMicroservices can also make sense when an organization has many teams working on different parts of the system. For example: Team A → User Service , Team B → Post Service \nEach team can own and deploy its service independently. With a huge monolithic codebase, many teams working on the same codebase can lead to: More coordination , More merge conflicts , More dependencies between teams , More difficult deployments. \nMicroservices can help teams work more independently. However, team size alone doesn't automatically mean you need microservices. A modular monolith can also solve many of these problems.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2YzL6EPf2jRdDFJiOeK2O",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5341.113081536986,
      "y": 814.4526230672225,
      "width": 2763.8106666666686,
      "height": 583.0498229043683,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "apx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1820243550,
      "version": 2028,
      "versionNonce": 861965826,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790620371687,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Downsides of Microservices\n\nMicroservices give us flexibility, but they also introduce complexity.\n\n1. Network Communication :- In a monolith: Service A → Service B may simply be an in-process function call. In microservices: Service A -->\nNetwork --> Service B The communication happens over a network, which introduces latency, network failures, retries, timeouts, etc.\n\n2. More Infrastructure :- Instead of managing one application, we may have to manage: Multiple services , Multiple deployments , Service-to-\nservice communication , Monitoring , Logging , Service discovery , Network configuration\nIf something fails, we need to determine which service failed and how the request moved through the system.\n\nSo microservices can significantly increase operational complexity.",
      "fontSize": 38.86998819362456,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Downsides of Microservices\n\nMicroservices give us flexibility, but they also introduce complexity.\n\n1. Network Communication :- In a monolith: Service A → Service B may simply be an in-process function call. In microservices: Service A --> Network --> Service B The communication happens over a network, which introduces latency, network failures, retries, timeouts, etc.\n\n2. More Infrastructure :- Instead of managing one application, we may have to manage: Multiple services , Multiple deployments , Service-to-service communication , Monitoring , Logging , Service discovery , Network configuration\nIf something fails, we need to determine which service failed and how the request moved through the system.\n\nSo microservices can significantly increase operational complexity.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "lnKwNt8JN_eOnByM33yL_",
      "type": "arrow",
      "x": 2687.08740046602,
      "y": 3056.3376234610632,
      "width": 1.7901673269398088,
      "height": 46.544350500441396,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
